WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.749 --> 00:00:16.359
What I'll be presenting on today is work that
I came into

00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:19.310
that was already ongoing, Linda Niccolai,

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:20.610
who was

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:24.849
one of my mentors during my postdoc along
with Robert Heimer,

00:00:24.849 --> 00:00:28.650
both of them are co-investigators, co-PIs on

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:32.460
a study of injection drug users,

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:37.500
gay men and and their sexual partners in St. Petersburg, Russia.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.010
And I have to say Russia because people
would think I mean Florida otherwise.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.320
So I did not design the study. I cannot take credit

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.530
for its creativity, for its insight.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.970
I'm analyzing the data, so I'm here to present

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:57.140
really mostly their work.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.470
So, the first thing you see about this slide probably
is that it's about Russia, and we don't hear

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.280
a lot about HIV in Russia, so what the heck
were we doing there?

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.210
St. Petersburg is a lovely place.
I'll give you the little Lonely Planet version

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:10.970
of it

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.710
in just a couple seconds. It was built,

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:19.140
it was commissioned pretty much in the early
eighteenth century by Peter the Great.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.240
It's called the Venice of the Baltic 'cause
it's built on forty two little

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.770
islands and islets separated by canals,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.010
and you can take cool

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:29.220
like Vaporetti through it, and it's

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.130
incredibly beautiful, and

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.010
it's like done in this

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:35.040
totally Baroque style.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.760
It's a trippy place. It's where the Hermitage is.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.869
So why are we in Russia? 

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:48.080
Why'd we go all the way there to study HIV?
We have plenty of HIV in North America.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.369
There's a lot of HIV in the developing world

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:52.909
that we know of.

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:54.149
This might seem

00:01:54.149 --> 00:01:57.550
like kind of an esoteric place to be doing
it,

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:02.050
but you know, I'm an epidemiologist. I want
to go where the disease is. That's why I work

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.749
with sexually transmitted infections among

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:07.560
homosexually active men because

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.950
we're number one with the bullet with HIV in
this country. We're the ones who get HIV more

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.849
than anyone else. That's true in a lot of countries,

00:02:14.849 --> 00:02:17.169
so what's the interest in Russia?

00:02:17.169 --> 00:02:20.020
Epidemiologists…you're all

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:23.549
in the public health program, so you know
what this is,

00:02:23.549 --> 00:02:25.129
the water pump

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:29.259
outside the John Snow Pub  in London. It's
like, we've got water pumps here. What are we going

00:02:29.259 --> 00:02:31.279
all the way to Russia to find a

00:02:31.279 --> 00:02:33.689
problematic water pump for?

00:02:33.689 --> 00:02:37.579
I'll give you a little background on Russia, and
it'll lead up to why we're doing an HIV study

00:02:37.579 --> 00:02:38.569
there.

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:40.629
Russia has been…can you hear me okay?

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:44.479
Okay. Russia's been through a lot in the
twentieth century. Unless you just

00:02:44.479 --> 00:02:46.759
beamed down from Mars, you know that.

00:02:46.759 --> 00:02:48.319
It started in the

00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:51.240
early twentieth century as

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.390
a Czarist state, which had only recently
gotten rid of serfdom. It was feudalism.

00:02:57.029 --> 00:03:01.619
Most of the citizens were not citizens. They
were actually slaves of their vassals.

00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:07.249
And then went through a revolution…World
War I, a révolution,

00:03:07.249 --> 00:03:13.999
World War II, which killed thirty million
Russians. This is a memorial…a carved relief

00:03:13.999 --> 00:03:16.510
by one of the two places in the Neva river,

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.069
which when frozen during the two

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:21.929
winters that it was blockaded

00:03:21.929 --> 00:03:23.789
by the Nazi army,

00:03:23.789 --> 00:03:25.579
which was

00:03:25.579 --> 00:03:27.909
one of two places in the city where you could
get water

00:03:27.909 --> 00:03:29.530
with your bucket and then

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:32.659
run home hoping that a sniper didn't pick
you off while you

00:03:32.659 --> 00:03:33.799
got home. So

00:03:33.799 --> 00:03:38.129
these are kind of tough people. They've
been through a lot.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:40.069
What brought us

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:44.359
to HIV is things that have happened in
the more recent past.

00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:49.879
In 1989 through '91
really was the period of transition

00:03:49.879 --> 00:03:51.430
from Marxism

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:56.019
to a capitalist economy and a huge

00:03:56.019 --> 00:03:57.479
number,

00:03:57.479 --> 00:04:02.539
a great deal of turmoil happened with that.
It's you know, for the Americans in the

00:04:02.539 --> 00:04:06.499
audience, we're used to a very stable
form of government here.

00:04:06.499 --> 00:04:08.279
Imagine that changed

00:04:08.279 --> 00:04:10.489
within a short period of time.

00:04:10.489 --> 00:04:13.339
That was a difficult transition period, but
what was even a

00:04:13.339 --> 00:04:18.239
bigger deal was the economic collapse that
came in 1994.

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:21.229
As my colleagues in St. Petersburg,

00:04:21.229 --> 00:04:24.189
as one has said to me,

00:04:24.189 --> 00:04:28.749
we thought it was hard to get bread and milk
in the grocery stores under communism,

00:04:28.749 --> 00:04:32.930
but then after the economic collapse it
was just ridiculous.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.199
Elderly people just stopped receiving pension
checks,

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:43.920
paychecks were no longer being cut even
though people were

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.949
showing up to the factory for work every day
and every week,

00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:48.150
the system,

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:54.210
the infrastructure of the country basically
fell apart.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.530
It's one of the bizarre cases

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:02.830
that we know of where like expectancy for a
country actually went down

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.180
during the early 1990s.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.269
That's pretty much unprecedented

00:05:08.269 --> 00:05:12.289
except in some in some places in sub-Saharan
Africa due to HIV.

00:05:12.289 --> 00:05:15.809
This was primarily due to other factors.

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:19.189
"The striking rise in Russian mortality 

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:23.270
is beyond the peacetime experience of industrialized
countries with a

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:27.260
five-year decline in life expectancy in four years time.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:31.229
Many factors appear to be operating simultaneously including
economic and social

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:33.020
instability,

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:37.819
high rates of tobacco and alcohol consumption,
poor nutrition, depression

00:05:37.819 --> 00:05:40.150
and deterioration of the health care system."

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.090
That appeared in JAMA

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:42.520
about

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.150
twelve years ago.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:50.280
I would throw in there too the interpersonal
violence and suicide also took a

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:53.319
great number of lives, particularly those of
men

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:54.319
during the

00:05:54.319 --> 00:05:54.860
post-

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.380
Soviet period.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:01.720
Okay, what's that got to do with HIV?

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.560
It's almost like a war.

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.740
More people die of infectious diseases following
a war or during the war than

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:09.759
usually die

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:11.999
from bullets or shrapnel.

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:14.139
With turmoil

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:15.320
comes ill health

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:19.269
effects, and this is kind of a case study in that.

00:06:19.269 --> 00:06:20.740
This appeared by,

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:24.330
he's got an Armenian name, but he's a Russian writer,

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:25.449
in the 

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:29.909
Journal of STD and AIDS, and I think it
sounds a little bit like a moral panic,

00:06:29.909 --> 00:06:31.599
what he's getting to here,

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:36.470
but it also frames the issue, I think. "Fundamental
cultural and social

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:39.650
and economic transitions happened during this
period.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.199
Sustained economic

00:06:42.199 --> 00:06:44.629
crises worsened the quality of life.

00:06:44.629 --> 00:06:47.729
We have poverty, joblessness,

00:06:47.729 --> 00:06:49.069
refugee migration and

00:06:49.069 --> 00:06:51.779
fatalism concerning the future.

00:06:51.779 --> 00:06:54.870
And we have social acceptance of prostitution,

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.349
which

00:06:57.349 --> 00:06:59.499
has its pros and cons.

00:06:59.499 --> 00:07:03.610
Temporary sexual relationships among young people
and declining age at first sex, and whether or

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:06.490
not you think these are

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:11.169
morally or ethically shiny and good for
you, they can have

00:07:11.169 --> 00:07:13.419
deleterious health effect.

00:07:13.419 --> 00:07:15.139
So,

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:17.149
the big thing that happened

00:07:17.149 --> 00:07:19.659
that concerns HIV

00:07:19.659 --> 00:07:21.560
during this period of turmoil

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.910
was remember you had a lot of out of work
people,

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:29.189
you had a lot of people who were not receiving
government stipends that they were used to

00:07:29.189 --> 00:07:29.669
including

00:07:29.669 --> 00:07:32.050
a huge number

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:37.530
of veterans of the Afghan war, many
of whom were disabled and didn't really have

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:40.110
a way of of surviving,

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.550
who took advantage of existing

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:45.360
drug trade routes coming in from

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:51.199
not the golden triangle of southeast Asia
but the golden triangle of southwest Asia.

00:07:51.199 --> 00:07:53.169
Following the Afghan war

00:07:53.169 --> 00:07:58.740
and following the turmoil that happened with
the Soviet excursion into Afghanistan in

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.939
1980 and its aftermath,

00:08:00.939 --> 00:08:06.389
a lot of poppies grown there, kind of like what we saw
with the Taliban not so long ago.

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:08.219
And

00:08:08.219 --> 00:08:13.439
black tar, heroin, which was similar to the stuff
that comes from Mexico,

00:08:13.439 --> 00:08:15.449
its very minimally processed,

00:08:15.449 --> 00:08:16.940
called chyorny,

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:18.179
which means black...

00:08:18.179 --> 00:08:21.129
It's essentially opium; it's

00:08:21.129 --> 00:08:22.149
gunk...

00:08:22.149 --> 00:08:24.750
was getting dragged into Russian cities

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:26.849
very very cheaply.

00:08:26.849 --> 00:08:28.120
And what happened

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:31.320
is a huge proportion of a birth cohort

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:33.100
became addicted to heroin.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:34.859
We know that in the United States

00:08:34.859 --> 00:08:39.910
the number one birth cohort year for picking up heroin was 1955.

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:42.270
And although it hasn't been

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:45.920
formally quantified, all the anecdotal evidence
suggests

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.530
that the years that people most picked up heroin,
the birth years, were

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:51.719
1978 

00:08:51.719 --> 00:08:53.740
to 1982.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:57.429
So you've got this bolus of folks who picked
up heroin

00:08:57.429 --> 00:08:59.500
moving to the demographic python

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.200
as time goes by.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:02.389
And they

00:09:02.389 --> 00:09:06.860
acquire and transmit HIV.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:09.380
Say, I'll just go to the next slide. 

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:13.620
So before we did this study what led us to do
 a study of HIV among injection drug

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:15.050
users in St Petersburg?

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:16.080
Well we had

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.650
some evidence that there was a problem.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:25.170
There were, you know, studies of varying validity
and generalizability that were showing

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:30.710
two to four percent of the IV users in the city
were infected in '98, then

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:33.960
eleven to eighteen percent by 2000,

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.520
thirty percent by 2002.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:38.480
Looks like we've got an emerging

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.210
infection problem here.

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:45.230
What we now know independent of this study
I'll be presenting here but that

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:49.459
contributes to it is Russia now has
one of the fastest-growing epidemics in the

00:09:49.459 --> 00:09:51.060
world.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:54.710
About one percent of the Russian population
is HIV infected.

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:58.650
That translates to half a million to 1.5 
million russians given

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:02.420
the appropriate confidence intervals around
everything.

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:08.070
But unlike many other countries, unlike other industrialized
countries, the vast majority of the infections

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:10.910
are among injection drug users.

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:16.810
We also saw some other indicators that injection
drug users were not the only population being

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:17.800
affected.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:18.590
Positive

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:21.519
test prevalence…HIV testing is done

00:10:21.519 --> 00:10:25.090
for almost all women who present at hospital
to give birth.

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:29.400
If they're getting prenatal care it's done then,
and if they haven't

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:30.479
had prenatal care

00:10:30.479 --> 00:10:33.370
they do a rapid test and stat they get her

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:35.560
the HAART that will prove that

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:37.740
neonatal transmission.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.820
The

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:43.240
positive test prevalence went from nothing in
1998

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:48.060
in the city to almost one and a half percent by
2002.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:52.130
Not huge numbers, but actually that's
a pretty high

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:56.170
positive test prevalence for pregnant women
in the developed world.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:01.149
The good thing is most, you know, nearly all
these women do get

00:11:01.149 --> 00:11:04.290
HAART...

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:07.040
nevirapine, rather.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.240
The rapid rise was what concerned us.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:14.850
Another population that did,
37 percent of

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:16.260
St Petersburg street youth

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.700
were found to be HIV infected.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:22.319
The problem was…it's clear

00:11:22.319 --> 00:11:24.080
that it's going to be

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:27.990
higher among those who inject, but
then it was also higher among those using

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.440
inhalants, and you don't get HIV from inhalants.

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:33.390
It was independently associated with being an orphan.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.940
And with being a double orphan more than a
single orphan,

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:41.840
and those aren't things that transmit HIV, so
clearly social disruption was playing 

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:43.810
a part in this.

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:46.470
And then what led directly to this study was

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:48.180
was they noticed that between

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:52.230
2001 and 2003, the proportion

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:55.519
of newly diagnosed cases, newly reported cases,

00:11:55.519 --> 00:11:58.400
went from five to twenty percent,

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:00.390
which means…which was a

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.930
possible indicator that

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:08.400
HIV was no longer just being transmitted within
the core group of injection drug users, but

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:09.089
then

00:12:09.089 --> 00:12:11.620
spreading on to their sex partners, spilling over

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:13.910
to the other people at risk.

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:14.380
And

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:18.200
the question was will enough of this happen
to sustain

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:20.940
a generalized heterosexual epidemic

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:24.630
like you see in other areas of the world.

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:30.030
So this is the group that got together to
talk about those questions. There were

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:36.360
four sites in the US including us partnering 
with the biomedical center in St Petersburg,

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:41.230
the sexual acquisition transmission of
HIV cooperative agreement program,

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:47.470
to look at the role of sexual transmission
within cores,

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:48.679
meaning gay men

00:12:48.679 --> 00:12:54.639
and drug users. They included non-injection
drug users because of so much sexual transmission

00:12:54.639 --> 00:12:57.430
going on between crack cocaine users

00:12:57.430 --> 00:13:00.320
and to look at what the

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:02.450
overall epidemic effect would be

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:06.410
to people outside those groups.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:10.670
Sorry, didn't switch the slide around.

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:13.480
This is part of the background of why we did
this study because

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:15.940
the study included gay men

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:21.950
I gave you background on IDUs, on other
populations in St Petersburg,

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.550
now a little late I'll give you

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:27.389
the information that we had about gay men
in Russia

00:13:27.389 --> 00:13:32.090
prior to doing this study, which was basically
jack.

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:35.760
Pretty much everything I mean you know you
go to the latest

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.850
information on the Euro surveillance website.

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:41.540
Eurosurveillance dot

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:42.990
euro or something.

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:48.300
The latest thing they've got for
Russia is one small study, four hundred guys,

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:50.090
total convenience sample

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:53.730
of guys showing up at gay bars. They
didn't even weight them

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:55.820
by how often they went to bars,

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:59.580
so it's really just a study of how many people
were standing around a bar at any

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:01.360
given moment.

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.130
Not the most methodologically strong

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:06.750
survey, but it found one and a half percent

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:09.690
had reported an HIV-positive test.

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:13.389
This was pretty much all we
knew about gay men and HIV in Russia

00:14:13.389 --> 00:14:14.500
to that point.

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:16.320
But

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:17.759
thirty seven

00:14:17.759 --> 00:14:19.900
percent had not used a condom

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:22.180
for their last act of anal sex.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:24.460
Huge proportion of sex trade.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:25.600
About one in five

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:28.650
had either paid for sex or had been paid for
sex.

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:31.770
Thirty seven percent also had female partners.

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:33.990
So there's a lot of

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:34.540
potential,

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:36.869
not a lot of HIV, but a lot of

00:14:36.869 --> 00:14:39.250
potential for transmission within the population

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:42.550
and then outside the population.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:45.930
So that made us want to study gay men in Russia
too.

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:51.380
The methods that … used, it was pretty much a 
cross-sectional study.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:56.630
Between 2005 and 2008, they sampled
from the two core groups

00:14:56.630 --> 00:15:02.070
and also their male and female sexual partners.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.270
So

00:15:03.270 --> 00:15:06.710
we had two core respondent-driven sampling,

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:09.710
respondent-driven sampling

00:15:09.710 --> 00:15:11.820
begins with seed participants.

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:13.800
Like let's say you're going to be

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:16.430
in a study of public health students, and

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:19.549
you get into the study the traditional way
by like

00:15:19.549 --> 00:15:21.500
maybe answering an ad.

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:23.230
What happens then is

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:24.629
you're asked to recruit

00:15:24.629 --> 00:15:28.610
other members of your population because you
know those people,

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:29.179
and

00:15:29.179 --> 00:15:32.010
because you got paid twenty bucks to be in
the study,

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:36.339
they'll pay the other person you recruit
twenty bucks, but then they'll give you five

00:15:36.339 --> 00:15:39.860
because you successfully recruited
somebody else.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:41.970
It's not so much to twist your arm

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:44.019
to twist other people's arms,

00:15:44.019 --> 00:15:47.520
but it's enough to say thanks and make you
not feel

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:50.050
taken advantage of if you actually do it.

00:15:50.050 --> 00:15:52.220
It differs from a snowball sample on that

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:54.359
at each step, with each recruitment,

00:15:54.359 --> 00:15:56.939
you weight by the inclusion probabilities.

00:15:56.939 --> 00:15:58.370
So you say if

00:15:58.370 --> 00:16:02.670
eight of the ten other public health students
that you know are female,

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:05.740
but the one person you recruited was male,

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:09.810
that's kind of not random recruitment, so

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:11.670
you can do

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:15.810
through the magic of mathematical waiting

00:16:15.810 --> 00:16:16.710
allegedly

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:18.620
with long enough recruitment chains,

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:20.230
you can get your sample to

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:23.220
emulate a probability sample.

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:24.420
But, yeah right.

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:26.350
It doesn't really happen

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:28.570
in real life. It's a lot better in theory.

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:30.320
It turns out

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:34.639
that RDS seems to be a better recruitment tool
than a sampling tool.

00:16:34.639 --> 00:16:39.309
It's somewhere between just a crude snowball
sample and an actual probability sample,

00:16:39.309 --> 00:16:41.610
and that's why we're hosting a

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:44.779
small symposium in the spring called RDS 2.0

00:16:44.779 --> 00:16:46.660
to see what we can do

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:48.760
with technique.

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:53.210
Blood specimens to test for HIV were tested…

00:16:53.210 --> 00:16:57.030
we ran ELISAs and Western blots to
confirm the positives.

00:16:57.030 --> 00:17:00.319
I don't have it on this slide, but we also tested for...

00:17:00.319 --> 00:17:05.740
we did syphilis serologies, tested for gonorrhea, chlamydia,

00:17:07.870 --> 00:17:09.320
and HEP C and B.

00:17:10.330 --> 00:17:14.350
We used anonymous structured questionnaires
using CASI,

00:17:14.350 --> 00:17:17.110
which is basically that you take your

00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:19.170
questionnaire on a laptop

00:17:19.170 --> 00:17:20.799
instead of paper and pencil,

00:17:20.799 --> 00:17:25.869
and we asked for information on sexual risk behaviors

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:27.319
and then information about

00:17:27.319 --> 00:17:30.440
up to five most recent sexual partners

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:34.390
and got the information about social networks
that I described earlier that you needed to

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:35.990
do the weighting.

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:36.950
And here's

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:38.600
what we got. We got

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:42.280
669 IDUs, 231

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:47.630
homosexually active men, and those don't overlap
because the guys who did overlap those

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:48.710
two groups

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:49.830
were 22,

00:17:49.830 --> 00:17:51.300
all of 22, so...

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:54.000
We don't have a lot of power to look

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.730
at homosexually active men here, but

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:57.950
we have more,

00:17:57.950 --> 00:18:03.360
and we have probably a better sample
of data than in previous studies.

00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:04.580
The HIV...
0:18:04.580,0:18:06.940
the prevalence of HIV antibody

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:12.790
among the injection drug users is huge. It's 44 percent.

00:18:12.790 --> 00:18:14.250
That's a lot.

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:16.380
That was higher

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:21.570
even as a follow up to thirty.
That's a huge number of people.

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:23.600
Among

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:28.520
homosexually active men who didn't inject
drugs, it was 7 percent,

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:30.049
which is

00:18:30.049 --> 00:18:33.360
definitely present. It's about
half

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:36.740
what you would see in a city its size
in the United States where...

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:40.650
or are about three-quarters of what you would
see in Europe.

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:42.200
What's interesting here is

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:45.960
the gay men who injected drugs looked a lot
more like...

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:49.990
like the drug injectors who weren't gay men

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:52.490
than the gay men who didn't inject.

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:53.480
So

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:55.080
their risk

00:18:55.080 --> 00:18:55.850
is

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:59.309
you know, possibly coming from injection rather than sex.

00:18:59.309 --> 00:19:01.020
We don't know yet.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:02.690
Hepatitis C:

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:04.850
nearly universal,

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:08.370
nearly ubiquitous among the people who injected

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:14.420
and a heavy burden of STIs among anybody.
I mean, you screen any group of

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:16.050
people, and you get

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:20.670
prevalence for bacterial sexually transmitted
infections of six, eight and fourteen percent.

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:25.080
That's a lot of people with chlamydia or gonorrhea
on board.

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:27.600
The proportion

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:31.840
of the people who were positive who were previously
diagnosed

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:34.639
is pretty low. In this country

00:19:34.639 --> 00:19:41.190
what proportion of people with HIV don't
know if they have HIV? Do you know?

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:42.770
Yeah, it's about twenty percent.

00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:44.650
Yeah, here

00:19:44.650 --> 00:19:45.410
we saw

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:50.480
the proportion who were previously diagnosed was
42 with the injection drug users, so

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:51.139
it was

00:19:51.139 --> 00:19:51.960
fifty eight

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:53.089
who didn't know

00:19:53.089 --> 00:19:54.780
they had HIV.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:20:00.410
Sixty nine percent of the MSM didn't
know they had HIV, so

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:02.470
much, much lower

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:05.539
coverage with testing even though

00:20:05.539 --> 00:20:08.159
most of them had been tested in the past.

00:20:08.159 --> 00:20:09.730
Just wasn't enough.

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:15.240
So we looked at characteristics associated
with HIV infection and risky sex

00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:16.090
for

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:17.730
both groups.

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.919
I'll just present it for

00:20:19.919 --> 00:20:21.929
HIV infection

00:20:21.929 --> 00:20:23.380
for IDs today.

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:25.240
We

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:26.840
controlled for a bunch of

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:30.150
potential covariates. We didn't want to take it to far
because we didn't want it to 

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:32.620
be a fishing expedition,

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:33.360
because

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:35.659
we didn't have that much n to work with

00:20:35.659 --> 00:20:38.059
and I didn't want any spurious

00:20:38.059 --> 00:20:41.240
p values. But these are the usual suspects.

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:44.120
I'll just give you the adjusted odds ratios. 

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:45.719
This is the odds, 

00:20:45.719 --> 00:20:50.000
these are the adjusted odds ratios for testing
HIV positive

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:53.310
if you were a non-gay injection drug user

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:54.400
in Russia.

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:57.800
The women were more likely to test positive.

00:20:57.800 --> 00:21:00.260
The people who were…who had

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:02.770
active employment, less.

00:21:02.770 --> 00:21:06.460
People who were...who had a history of incarceration,
more.

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:09.690
And people who resided with their family

00:21:09.690 --> 00:21:12.710
were more likely to test positive, and that

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:14.399
is an interesting thing on a

00:21:14.399 --> 00:21:17.570
few different levels actually because

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:18.100
one,

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:21.190
it's an indicator of socio-economic status.

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:23.519
Who lives with their family when they're an
adult?

00:21:23.519 --> 00:21:27.239
Well, people who don't have the money to live
on their own because no one in their right

00:21:27.239 --> 00:21:28.850
mind would do that by choice.

00:21:29.789 --> 00:21:30.529
And

00:21:30.529 --> 00:21:32.000
it also means that you don't

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:36.060
get to shoot up in the kitchen table, do you? You know?

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:40.340
You're not going to do it in front of mama and papa.

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:41.970
You know, you're a...

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:43.380
it's just not going to work.

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:44.639
So

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:48.660
it's associated in some

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:52.350
studies with unhygenic

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:53.410
injection practices

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:58.520
because you're doing it on the sly.

00:21:58.520 --> 00:22:00.110
We looked at the

00:22:00.110 --> 00:22:04.410
homosexually active men where we included
the IDs in here because we needed to conserve

00:22:04.410 --> 00:22:05.820
n by any means possible,

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:07.830
and

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:12.100
HIV infection was associated with being

00:22:12.100 --> 00:22:12.920
older than...

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:15.820
age twenty four or older,

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:18.850
having a history of drug injection,

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:21.799
and negatively associated with full- or part-time

00:22:21.799 --> 00:22:25.870
employment.

00:22:25.870 --> 00:22:29.580
Things changed a little because we had this
funny situation

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:33.070
where

00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:37.850
we had a fair number of men…we had about fifteen
guys.

00:22:37.850 --> 00:22:41.830
Everybody got a global question: do you have sex
with men? Do have sex with women? Or do you

00:22:41.830 --> 00:22:43.730
sex with both?

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:45.370
And about fifteen guys said

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:48.120
I only have sex with women.

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:49.170
And we had others who

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:52.810
told us they had sex with men and women, but

00:22:52.810 --> 00:22:55.420
this number said

00:22:55.420 --> 00:22:56.749
only women.

00:22:56.749 --> 00:23:00.460
Then they went on to tell us about the two
or three male sex partners that they'd had

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:04.050
when we asked them who the last people you'd
had sex with were.

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:06.210
But for

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:09.499
coding reasons, just for skip patterns
in the computer program,

00:23:09.499 --> 00:23:13.260
they didn't get questions about so the
last time you were with

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:14.459
this guy,

00:23:14.459 --> 00:23:16.510
you know, who you've given the initials

00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:18.340
TR or whatever,

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:24.070
what did you do with him? Did you have sex
with a condom? Did you have sex without a condom?

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:25.220
You know,

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:28.269
is it on the internet? You know, what did you
do?

00:23:28.269 --> 00:23:30.870
A ton of the invasive questions we would ask.

00:23:30.870 --> 00:23:34.770
And they didn't answer those
things because they weren't given the questions,

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:37.390
so we weren't able to assess

00:23:37.390 --> 00:23:39.400
their condom use, which was

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:40.390
highly annoying.

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:42.590
So what I did is I limited

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:48.640
this to...

00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:50.330
Well, guess what, okay.

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:54.080
I limited this. Actually this 253 is wrong.

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:57.000
It's actually down by about twenty,

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:59.000
no, by about fifteen.

00:24:00.179 --> 00:24:02.960
But rather than being an injection drug user,

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:05.050
the greatest risk

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:08.980
when I was able to assess what your
partners were like, like was that last guy

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:12.429
you had sex with a drug injector,

00:24:12.429 --> 00:24:15.190
having a partner who injected drugs

00:24:15.190 --> 00:24:15.970
was

00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:18.120
the highest risk factor.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:22.790
So what does that tell you? Well we go back
to the earlier slide where we saw that the

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:25.090
HIV prevalence was so much greater

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:29.120
among drug injectors including the gay men
among them

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:30.899
than among gay men.

00:24:30.899 --> 00:24:31.580
This is

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:32.740
a bizarre

00:24:32.740 --> 00:24:35.419
situation where we might actually see

00:24:35.419 --> 00:24:37.930
gay men getting HIV

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:40.159
from the injection drug users.

00:24:40.159 --> 00:24:45.700
That's just a postulate at this point. I'm
basing that on few observations, but

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:47.630
we shall see.

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:52.080
The prevalence of risky behavior

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:55.870
among these guys was high.

00:24:55.870 --> 00:24:57.690
About twenty percent of them

00:24:57.690 --> 00:24:59.210
had ever

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:03.440
participated in sex work, traded
sex for money or drugs.

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:08.740
About a third of them had had sex while high

00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:10.480
in the last six months with

00:25:10.480 --> 00:25:12.159
one partner or another.

00:25:13.790 --> 00:25:18.800
Almost half of them had had any unprotected
sex. And then when I limited it,

00:25:18.800 --> 00:25:19.440
I excluded...

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:24.180
Well, it's one thing for you to have unprotected
sex with somebody you met that night. It's

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:26.210
another thing for you to have unprotected
sex

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:30.059
with your partner of five years, and you've both 
been HIV tested and...

00:25:30.059 --> 00:25:34.330
I excluded those partnerships where they
knew the partner's status and it was a 

00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:35.380
main partner.

00:25:35.380 --> 00:25:37.070
Forty two percent of them

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:38.610
had had

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:39.880
unprotected sex that

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:42.380
is really risky for HIV.

00:25:42.380 --> 00:25:44.190
And the proportion

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:46.510
was greater

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:49.250
with all of these,

00:25:49.250 --> 00:25:51.110
I think.

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:53.799
No, it was greater for sex while high

00:25:53.799 --> 00:25:55.690
but was actually

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:56.510
lower

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:58.730
for unprotected anal sex

00:25:58.730 --> 00:26:00.770
for the injection drug users,

00:26:00.770 --> 00:26:02.460
yet they have for HIV,

00:26:02.460 --> 00:26:06.740
so where is the HIV coming from?

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:11.790
And then I broke down the number of partners
in the last six months with whom the last

00:26:11.790 --> 00:26:12.460
act of

00:26:12.460 --> 00:26:15.190
anal intercourse was unprotected.

00:26:15.190 --> 00:26:17.650
So it's not just this,

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:19.850
you know this

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:22.750
half of people have had unprotected sex

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:24.120
that's the problem.

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:28.170
You know, we had for forty seven percent had
no partners of this.

00:26:28.170 --> 00:26:31.620
Thirty two percent had one, but then twenty
percent

00:26:31.620 --> 00:26:32.619
of the people,

00:26:32.619 --> 00:26:35.770
of the guys in the study had had unprotected
anal sex

00:26:35.770 --> 00:26:38.180
with two guys or more

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:40.000
in the last six months.

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:42.810
So this is a hierarchy of risk here.

00:26:42.810 --> 00:26:44.280
We not just have

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:47.789
half the guys engaging in a little bit of risk here,
we have

00:26:47.789 --> 00:26:50.929
twenty percent of them engaging in a lot of
risk in

00:26:50.929 --> 00:26:53.520
a six-month period.

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:56.660
So the only

00:26:56.660 --> 00:26:59.360
independent association

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:02.000
for unprotected sex with a male partner

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:05.929
was HIV infection negatively associated.
The negative guys

00:27:05.929 --> 00:27:09.050
were much less likely to report

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:11.860
risky sex.

00:27:11.860 --> 00:27:17.630
Five of the HIV positive participants reported
unprotected sex with a total of six partners,

00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:21.850
but none of them had been previously diagnosed.

00:27:21.850 --> 00:27:23.570
And

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:25.519
none of them had been previously diagnosed.

00:27:25.519 --> 00:27:28.770
So we didn't have a single guy tell us

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:33.540
who knew he had HIV, right? We tested him for HIV,
we found out whether he had HIV. Have you ever had

00:27:33.540 --> 00:27:37.059
a positive test before? Oh yeah, two years
ago.

00:27:37.059 --> 00:27:38.710
None of those guys

00:27:38.710 --> 00:27:39.669
had told us

00:27:39.669 --> 00:27:40.980
that they had

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:42.090
unprotected sex

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:44.170
with another guy.

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:47.300
And we're thinking wow, that's really low.

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:48.380
But in Russia

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:50.539
the laws against

00:27:50.539 --> 00:27:54.220
knowingly exposing someone to your HIV are severe.

00:27:54.220 --> 00:27:57.900
So we're wondering whether that can be some
reporting bias.

00:27:57.900 --> 00:27:58.459
However,

00:27:58.459 --> 00:28:00.620
we found

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:04.530
when we looked at female partnerships

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:09.289
that the greatest predictor of having unprotected
sex

00:28:09.289 --> 00:28:11.960
with a female partner was having a history of drug

00:28:11.960 --> 00:28:12.890
injection.

00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:14.350
It's the IDUs

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:17.580
who are crossing over to the females by and
large,

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:20.190
but four percent of them

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:24.350
reported unprotected sex with female partners.

00:28:25.290 --> 00:28:30.310
Also four participants, all four participants
were IDUs. None of these 

00:28:30.310 --> 00:28:32.010
were gay men.

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:37.079
They been previously diagnosed…three had
been previously diagnosed with HIV.

00:28:37.079 --> 00:28:42.260
So these guys knew they had HIV and were
having unprotected sex with their female partners.

00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:43.590
The funky thing is

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:47.679
of the four women they exposed, all were IDUs,

00:28:47.679 --> 00:28:48.570
and two

00:28:48.570 --> 00:28:50.690
of those had already told

00:28:50.690 --> 00:28:54.530
the participant that they were HIV-positive.

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:55.700
So

00:28:55.700 --> 00:28:58.110
maybe these guys are making a decision

00:28:58.110 --> 00:29:00.799
about which women to expose

00:29:00.799 --> 00:29:01.210
because

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:02.360
it's like well

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:06.360
she had already has HIV, so that's one thing,
or

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:09.130
she's an IDU.

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:11.120
Maybe the decision is to

00:29:11.120 --> 00:29:12.919
knowingly

00:29:12.919 --> 00:29:15.130
expose an IDU partner more than

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:17.410
a non-IDU partner.

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:20.390
That's speculation. That's the question that
it raises.

00:29:20.390 --> 00:29:24.960
So what does this tell us about HIV among
drug injectors in St Petersburg?

00:29:26.700 --> 00:29:27.990
A lot of HIV,

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:31.460
particularly compared to what they had
had a few years earlier.

00:29:31.460 --> 00:29:33.100
Many times

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:35.480
what the prevalence is in western Europe.

00:29:35.480 --> 00:29:39.410
It's similar to what we saw in Harlem and the
south Bronx

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:42.470
at the height of the HIV epidemic here

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:47.729
or at height of the HIV epidemic what
we saw among gay men in San Francisco.

00:29:47.729 --> 00:29:49.880
These guys are getting clobbered.

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:53.170
And there are other indicators of rapid transmission
that are

00:29:53.170 --> 00:29:55.450
kind of independent from these data.

00:29:55.450 --> 00:29:59.560
For instance they were all of the same genotype,
but that's another talk.

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:02.280
A nice thing is it allowed us to estimate

00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:06.290
the number of active drug injectors in St Petersburg,

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:07.750
about eighty thousand

00:30:07.750 --> 00:30:10.010
give or take three thousand

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:13.290
was a piece that Robert Heimer and I just put out.

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:18.130
That's a lot. If half of those people have
HIV

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:23.720
and it's been going up the way it has, it makes
you wonder at what point this will begin to

00:30:23.720 --> 00:30:24.350
saturate

00:30:24.350 --> 00:30:26.909
because we really never saw

00:30:26.909 --> 00:30:30.820
HIV prevalence above fifty percent
in the United States.

00:30:30.820 --> 00:30:34.900
So will it start to slow down?

00:30:34.900 --> 00:30:35.960
We don't know.

00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:39.630
What does it tell us about homosexually active
men in the city?

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:42.940
The first caveat, the first thing I said when I showed
you this slide is that

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:47.270
we have a small n and our sampling strategy

00:30:47.270 --> 00:30:48.950
is limited.

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:54.610
The prevalence of HIV infection is about three-quarters
what it is in western Europe,

00:30:54.610 --> 00:30:57.159
which means given the size of the study

00:30:57.159 --> 00:30:59.029
it's not that different.

00:30:59.029 --> 00:31:01.429
The prevalence of risk behavior

00:31:01.429 --> 00:31:05.340
is about the same. About thirty seven
percent of these guys

00:31:05.340 --> 00:31:08.870
were taking…no, forty two percent these guys
were taking serious risk.

00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:12.150
That's pretty close to what you would see
in Spain or France.

00:31:12.150 --> 00:31:17.059
And with HIV seroincidence among gay men
it's about two percent in western

00:31:17.059 --> 00:31:22.760
Europe, North America, New Zealand. It's a
little less in Australia.

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:24.070
We can

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:25.290
wonder whether

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:27.799
we're having a similar seroincidence there.

00:31:27.799 --> 00:31:30.800
The number of new infections per hundred years

00:31:30.800 --> 00:31:34.510
of infected person time.

00:31:34.510 --> 00:31:37.570
Here's the funky thing though. In the west,

00:31:37.570 --> 00:31:39.570
current prevalence was preceded

00:31:39.570 --> 00:31:41.440
by a much greater prevalence.

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:43.119
Ten, fifteen years ago

00:31:43.119 --> 00:31:47.639
we had a very high HIV prevalence among gay
men in the United States. It went down for

00:31:47.639 --> 00:31:49.400
a while because those guys died.

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:53.640
Because it was before the time of HAART. 

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:56.270
If HIV

00:31:56.270 --> 00:32:00.390
prevalence among gay men in Russia has been
very low until recently

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:02.460
and we've got these high rates

00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:06.600
of unprotected sexual contact between
them,

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:07.640
it's

00:32:07.640 --> 00:32:09.829
emerging into a pretty…

00:32:09.829 --> 00:32:14.199
I don't want to say naive population because that
means you've never been exposed before. So I'm being 

00:32:14.199 --> 00:32:17.789
a good epidemiologist.

00:32:17.789 --> 00:32:23.510
But what it means is there could be an awful lot
of susceptible guys to infect.

00:32:23.510 --> 00:32:26.419
So the incidence could actually wind up being
greater

00:32:26.419 --> 00:32:30.000
than what it is in western Europe.

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:32.470
So

00:32:32.470 --> 00:32:34.850
if the prevalence until recently

00:32:34.850 --> 00:32:36.140
has been low

00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:38.310
in Russia what does this mean

00:32:38.310 --> 00:32:40.010
with this rate of contact

00:32:40.010 --> 00:32:42.019
and the current rate of prevalence

00:32:42.019 --> 00:32:45.870
if that's a new rate of prevalence, if that's an
emerging infection?

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:47.309
So we'll have to do more work

00:32:47.309 --> 00:32:51.100
to get to that.

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:57.559
It also tells us that we have some questions
about bisexuality, drug injection, and sex work.

00:32:57.559 --> 00:33:03.300
We didn't have a lot of information on
the bisexual guys. The ones

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:06.730
who didn't provide it said they were heterosexual.

00:33:06.730 --> 00:33:10.309
It's like the t-shirt that was running
around in the nineties that guys wore that said

00:33:10.309 --> 00:33:14.020
I'm not gay, but my boyfriend is.

00:33:14.020 --> 00:33:16.530
It's weird. It's like who were these other partners?

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:18.620
We had a great

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:21.250
problem getting data on sex work

00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:22.540
in this study.

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:25.039
The sex work questions were a little...

00:33:25.039 --> 00:33:26.900
were a little dodgy.

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:27.840
So what

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:30.909
we're kind of wondering is whether

00:33:30.909 --> 00:33:35.529
these guys who said I have no female partners
meanwhile I'll tell you I had sex with male

00:33:35.529 --> 00:33:38.980
partner A and male partner B, but I won't tell
you what I did with them,

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:44.270
we're wondering whether those were sex partners,
or trade sex partners.

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:45.090
And that

00:33:45.090 --> 00:33:49.620
this might indicate a lot of our homosexually
active IDUs

00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:50.629
trading sex

00:33:50.629 --> 00:33:52.780
with men for money or drugs

00:33:52.780 --> 00:33:55.320
and not getting recorded as that.

00:33:55.320 --> 00:34:01.690
And they could be a source of infection for
both male and female partners.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:04.309
So in order to address these

00:34:04.309 --> 00:34:07.290
situations we need better data on gay men

00:34:07.290 --> 00:34:11.669
in Russia, and we're doing… you know, it's a
really changing topography.

00:34:11.669 --> 00:34:13.709
This slide I look at, and I go

00:34:13.709 --> 00:34:15.299
you know,

00:34:15.299 --> 00:34:20.579
what's emerging in Russia is that they have
the opportunities for gay night life

00:34:20.579 --> 00:34:23.939
every bit as lame as we have in the United
States.

00:34:23.939 --> 00:34:28.609
This is Central Station. It's a gay bar
in St Petersburg, and I got to

00:34:28.609 --> 00:34:30.799
tell you, I was there in May,

00:34:30.799 --> 00:34:33.769
and the thing that people kept impressing
on me is

00:34:33.769 --> 00:34:39.379
this bar could't have been here three years ago.
This couldn't have existed. This is totally new.

00:34:39.379 --> 00:34:42.469
That all these people you see up here dancing and
having fun, 

00:34:42.469 --> 00:34:46.559
this is...

00:34:46.559 --> 00:34:50.249
really did feel exciting. It's a
watershed.

00:34:50.249 --> 00:34:54.239
The other thing that they've got now, is you see
in the lower left is you can

00:34:54.239 --> 00:34:55.949
meet a partner online.

00:34:55.949 --> 00:35:00.239
You know, it's easier to find sex online
than it is Dominoes.

00:35:01.799 --> 00:35:06.529
And they've got Russian language
websites right now, and

00:35:06.529 --> 00:35:07.380
and you can just dial it up,

00:35:07.380 --> 00:35:08.380
have someone

00:35:08.380 --> 00:35:10.569
in your bed that afternoon.

00:35:10.569 --> 00:35:16.349
So what that raises the question of is if
seroprevalence got to seven

00:35:16.349 --> 00:35:17.080
percent

00:35:17.080 --> 00:35:18.829
without gay bars

00:35:18.829 --> 00:35:22.460
and without gay internet hookup sites,

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:25.559
and thirty seven percent of the guys

00:35:25.559 --> 00:35:28.439
having unprotected sex in the last six months,

00:35:28.439 --> 00:35:29.649
or forty two, rather,

00:35:29.649 --> 00:35:31.289
What's it gonna be

00:35:31.289 --> 00:35:36.709
with the advent of these new methods of meeting
people?

00:35:36.709 --> 00:35:39.439
We just change the whole structure.

00:35:40.369 --> 00:35:42.999
There's another thing

00:35:42.999 --> 00:35:44.180
that we see, which is

00:35:44.180 --> 00:35:47.180
in the post-Soviet era I talked about earlier,

00:35:47.180 --> 00:35:50.180
female sex work and trafficking in women

00:35:50.180 --> 00:35:51.969
increased along with

00:35:51.969 --> 00:35:54.189
transmission risk.

00:35:54.189 --> 00:35:56.209
But it

00:35:56.209 --> 00:35:58.509
looks like sex work among men

00:35:58.509 --> 00:36:02.769
might be transmitting too, might be increasing too.
This picture in the lower left

00:36:02.769 --> 00:36:07.059
I took from gaytravel.ru, which which
is a web site

00:36:07.059 --> 00:36:09.849
where if you want to go on sex travel

00:36:09.849 --> 00:36:12.049
like you can to Thailand,

00:36:12.049 --> 00:36:14.439
you can go to Russia.

00:36:14.439 --> 00:36:18.439
And you know, this charming guy on the left here 
I'm sure is with the other one because

00:36:18.439 --> 00:36:22.229
of his great personality.

00:36:22.229 --> 00:36:24.069
You know, it's like the cheesiest

00:36:24.069 --> 00:36:27.749
picture I've ever seen. It's so scary.

00:36:27.749 --> 00:36:32.619
So if that's an emerging thing, that's going to
affect the transmission

00:36:32.619 --> 00:36:34.249
as well.

00:36:34.249 --> 00:36:37.559
So how are we going to study this stuff?

00:36:39.359 --> 00:36:41.930
It raises issues of controlling transmission.

00:36:41.930 --> 00:36:44.139
I'll jump back to drug users.

00:36:45.019 --> 00:36:46.630
We've got challenges

00:36:46.630 --> 00:36:50.589
in both these areas, in addressing transmission among
drug users

00:36:50.589 --> 00:36:53.029
and gay men.

00:36:53.029 --> 00:36:54.320
Right now in Russia

00:36:54.320 --> 00:36:55.869
if you're addicted

00:36:55.869 --> 00:36:58.489
to heroin, you've really got one mode

00:36:58.489 --> 00:37:00.769
of getting help, which is

00:37:00.769 --> 00:37:05.829
you know, the narcologists, the addiction specialists
there are going to

00:37:05.829 --> 00:37:07.779
lock you up,

00:37:07.779 --> 00:37:09.929
and you're going to withdraw,

00:37:09.929 --> 00:37:10.589
which is

00:37:10.589 --> 00:37:12.499
a painful and dangerous way

00:37:12.499 --> 00:37:14.169
to get off heroin, which is what

00:37:14.169 --> 00:37:16.390
the injection drug users use there.

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:19.309
There's no drug substitution there. It's illegal.

00:37:19.309 --> 00:37:20.250
You can't get methadone.

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:22.689
The doctor can't prescribe for you.

00:37:22.689 --> 00:37:25.630
Pharmacies can sell you syringes.

00:37:26.260 --> 00:37:29.829
They don't have needle exchange, but syringes
are legal. The only thing is

00:37:29.829 --> 00:37:32.390
every store you go into whether it's the

00:37:32.390 --> 00:37:36.869
electronics store I went in to get a 
new adaptor--I wanted a second adaptor

00:37:36.869 --> 00:37:38.520
for another plug, you know, that kind of thing.

00:37:38.520 --> 00:37:41.089
They all have off-duty cops

00:37:41.089 --> 00:37:42.310
moonlighting

00:37:42.310 --> 00:37:43.489
right by the door

00:37:43.489 --> 00:37:47.719
because it's a chaotic environment, and people
will just walk out with stuff otherwise.

00:37:47.719 --> 00:37:48.780
So if you're

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:51.239
a heroin addict,

00:37:51.239 --> 00:37:53.119
you really don't want to walk up

00:37:53.119 --> 00:37:53.640
to

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:55.269
the counter at

00:37:55.269 --> 00:37:57.169
their version of RiteAid

00:37:57.169 --> 00:37:58.990
and buy a pack of syringes.

00:37:58.990 --> 00:38:02.039
You can get beaten up when you leave.

00:38:02.039 --> 00:38:04.849
You can get harassed later on.

00:38:04.849 --> 00:38:07.699
And we know from

00:38:07.699 --> 00:38:11.910
another study that fear of police arrest for
carry the equipment like from

00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:14.119
a pharmacy with clean syringes

00:38:14.119 --> 00:38:16.459
is associated with risky injection

00:38:16.459 --> 00:38:18.420
in the Russian context.

00:38:18.420 --> 00:38:20.499
So this fear actually

00:38:20.499 --> 00:38:21.829
affects

00:38:21.829 --> 00:38:23.269
not just

00:38:23.269 --> 00:38:27.399
how we do it HIV prevention work

00:38:27.399 --> 00:38:31.099
but how we do HIV prevention research.

00:38:34.119 --> 00:38:37.919
Controlling transmission has some overall
challenges to it.

00:38:38.749 --> 00:38:40.770
Our work is is affected by

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:46.019
factors in both of our countries, especially
trying to do this cross-cultural work.

00:38:46.999 --> 00:38:49.019
Drug users are stigmatized.

00:38:49.019 --> 00:38:50.729
You see this like

00:38:50.729 --> 00:38:53.500
one of the few needle exchanges the happened
in Russia.

00:38:53.500 --> 00:38:55.439
That's a pretty grim place.

00:38:55.439 --> 00:38:56.700
Gay men

00:38:56.700 --> 00:39:01.330
are stigmatized, not prioritized, despite a huge
epidemiological burden

00:39:01.330 --> 00:39:03.089
in both countries.

00:39:03.089 --> 00:39:04.289
It's very difficult

00:39:04.289 --> 00:39:07.079
for any work with gay men to have legitimacy.

00:39:08.050 --> 00:39:10.290
The alarming picture that I have here,

00:39:10.290 --> 00:39:16.030
this guy tried to organize a gay pride parade 
in Moscow in 2009,

00:39:16.550 --> 00:39:17.769
and that's what the mayor

00:39:17.769 --> 00:39:19.969
had the cops do to him.

00:39:19.969 --> 00:39:24.150
So if you just want to waltz in as 
a researcher and say I want

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:29.319
do this important work with this population,

00:39:29.319 --> 00:39:31.050
in their country as in ours

00:39:31.050 --> 00:39:34.660
it might not get met with the best reception.

00:39:34.660 --> 00:39:35.449
Another

00:39:35.449 --> 00:39:40.119
real challenge to doing this kind of work
from the perspective of Yale or

00:39:40.119 --> 00:39:44.349
a US researcher or even researchers from the UK
or other areas

00:39:44.349 --> 00:39:45.629
is there's still

00:39:45.629 --> 00:39:50.180
a great deal…you know, our relationship with
Russia is still burdened by

00:39:50.180 --> 00:39:52.789
decades of mistrust in the past.

00:39:52.789 --> 00:39:55.489
You know, if you remember that song from the 80s. 

00:39:55.489 --> 00:39:59.679
But if you think it sounds…you know, what's
that old joke: I'm here from the United States

00:39:59.679 --> 00:40:01.149
government, and I'm here to help.

00:40:01.149 --> 00:40:03.399
It's like that sounds bad enough if you're
American,

00:40:03.399 --> 00:40:04.919
but can you imagine if

00:40:04.919 --> 00:40:06.390
you're Russian?

00:40:06.390 --> 00:40:07.189
Weren't you guys…

00:40:07.189 --> 00:40:10.489
didn't you used to have missiles pointing at us?

00:40:10.489 --> 00:40:13.710
It's a funky relationship.

00:40:13.710 --> 00:40:15.589
So those are all going to be

00:40:15.589 --> 00:40:18.290
difficulties that we're going to have to address.

00:40:18.290 --> 00:40:23.229
We know little about… you know, we know more about

00:40:23.229 --> 00:40:24.890
injection drug use

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:25.449
and

00:40:25.449 --> 00:40:26.559
HIV

00:40:26.559 --> 00:40:28.179
in Russia,

00:40:28.179 --> 00:40:31.549
a great deal more than we used to. Most of
what we know is bad.

00:40:31.549 --> 00:40:35.619
And we need to understand where things are
going to peak, whether they're going to, whether

00:40:35.619 --> 00:40:39.249
HIV is going to spill over into other populations, 

00:40:39.249 --> 00:40:39.730
and

00:40:39.730 --> 00:40:42.399
part of doing that work is stymied

00:40:42.399 --> 00:40:44.029
by

00:40:44.029 --> 00:40:46.329
difficult attitudes around drug use.

00:40:46.329 --> 00:40:48.959
The same thing is happening

00:40:48.959 --> 00:40:50.710
with doing work around gay men.

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:52.899
That's just in its infancy.

00:40:52.899 --> 00:40:54.539
But both of these things

00:40:54.539 --> 00:40:56.069
have a great

00:40:56.069 --> 00:40:58.479
potential for future spread.

00:40:58.479 --> 00:40:59.859
So I want to thank

00:40:59.859 --> 00:41:02.839
the people that I work with here at Yale

00:41:02.839 --> 00:41:06.919
on the study, to the good folks at the
biomedical institute who

00:41:06.919 --> 00:41:09.719
we work with. People with Yale CIRA.

00:41:09.719 --> 00:41:13.690
Thanks for the money, NIMH. We always

00:41:13.690 --> 00:41:17.099
appreciate it, and the good people at NGO Stellit.

00:41:17.099 --> 00:41:18.449
And you can't see it.

00:41:18.449 --> 00:41:20.479
No, it doesn't really show up well.

00:41:20.479 --> 00:41:21.690
This is the John Snow Pub,

00:41:21.690 --> 00:41:23.539
which is a great place to go for pint in

00:41:23.539 --> 00:41:26.279
the UK.

00:41:26.279 --> 00:41:30.649
And this is Robert Heimer who is the PI  of the study
along with our colleagues from Stellit

00:41:30.649 --> 00:41:32.259
at a swanky place in St Petersburg.

00:41:32.259 --> 00:41:34.409
So,

00:41:34.409 --> 00:41:35.149
I can take questions. Thanks.

